preConditionHook:
// A SMALL FAILED BREAKOUT. The PRIOR bar poked at least failTicks BELOW the low
// of the bar before it, and THIS bar trades back up through that level -- the
// five-or-nine-tick failed extension Brooks reports at the end of a protracted
// move, and a signal in the opposite direction. Written on the LONG side as the
// failed DOWNSIDE poke (the merged pseudocode states the short case); the short
// side is its mirror, a failed upside poke.
//
// EL: low[1] <= low[2] - failTicks * tickSize and high > low[2]
//
// THE REFERENCE LEVEL IS A FIXED BAR OFFSET, high[2] / low[2], not a remembered
// signal bar carried forward -- the mechanizable reading the merge chose.
//
// Not SmallBreakoutTrapReversal, which measures the poke against a 20-bar
// Lowest window in price points and requires the *closing price* back above it on
// the SAME bar; here the break and the recovery are on two DIFFERENT bars and
// the recovery is read from the high, so the two fire on different bars. Not
// StopRunReversalBar, which uses the same 20-bar level and a close term. Not
// NearMissLevelReversal, which is the deliberate opposite: price did NOT trade
// through the level.
//
// V00-24 gives 5 ticks and R24-01 gives 9 with a "near the end of a protracted
// trend" gate; the gate is dropped (it is the always-in / trend-duration read,
// a separate placement). failTicks is instrument-specific, like
// CloseNearPriorLow's threshold.
//
// The tick is written division-first, mirroring EL's MinMove / PriceScale, and
// read once per bar into a local. Every scratch value is a localVariables
// entry, never a hook-scope declaration, so the hooks survive a double
// placement. Deepest bar touched: 2.
tickSize = ctx.MinMove() / ctx.PriceScale();
